{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The audio-to-pose and audio-to-mesh networks are kept separate because pose is more closely associated with the rhythm and tone of the audio, which differs from the emphasis required for generating accurate 3D facial meshes. This separation allows each module to focus on the specific temporal and expressive cues relevant to its task, improving performance and accuracy. The distinct design ensures that the pose module captures rhythm and tone effectively, while the mesh module focuses on facial structure and expression. This architectural choice enhances the overall realism and consistency of the generated animations [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "pose is more closely associated with the rhythm and tone present in the audio, which is a different emphasis compared to the audio-to-mesh task"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "ARTalk generates one second of facial motion in 0.01 seconds on an NVIDIA Tesla A100 server GPU and 0.057 seconds on an Apple M2 Pro laptop chip, demonstrating high inference efficiency and low latency [S1]. The model required approximately 13 GPU hours for training, with 5 hours for the first stage and 8 hours for the second stage, indicating efficient resource utilization [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "During inference, our method needs only 0.01 seconds to generate motions for 1 second on an NVIDIA Tesla A100 GPU and 0.057 seconds on an Apple M2 Pro chip, showcasing high inference efficiency and low latency."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "All training was conducted on one NVIDIA Tesla A100 GPU, requiring a total of approximately 13 GPU hours (5 hours for the first stage and 8 hours for the second stage), demonstrating efficient training resource utilization."}
 ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "ARTalk's motion codebook consists of 256 entries, each with a code dimension of 64 [S2][S3]. It encodes a temporal window at multiscale levels [1, 5, 25, 50, 100] [S2][S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "The motion codebook consists of 256 entries, each with a code dimension of 64."},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "The time window size is 100 frames (4 seconds), and the multiscale levels are [1, 5, 25, 50, 100]."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "encodes motion sequences into multi-scale token maps [𝑀𝑘1 , 𝑀𝑘2 , ..., 𝑀𝐾 ] using a shared codebook and causal masking on temporal."}
 ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "ARTalk can animate a speaking style never seen during training by using a Transformer-based style encoder to extract style representations from example motion clips, enabling the model to generate stylized motions with unique individual characteristics and disentangle the complex many-to-many mapping between speech and motion [S4]. This design allows the model to adapt to unseen speaking styles, as demonstrated by its strong generalization ability on the VOCASET dataset without fine-tuning [S5].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "we integrate a Transformer-based style encoder to extract style representations from example motion clips. This allows our model to generate stylized motions with unique individual characteristics and helps to disentangle the complex many-to-many mapping between speech and motion."},
    {"source_id": "S5", "quote": "our method achieved highly competitive performance and outperformed most baseline methods specifically trained on this dataset. This indicates that our method surpasses them in terms of generalization ability, further verifying the robustness of our method in handling unseen styles and data."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Ethnicity labels for RAVDESS actors were obtained using FaRL [46], a CLIP-based model, classified into the same groups as in CREMA-D. Age labels were estimated using GPT-4 with a specific prompt asking for an integer age between 0 and 130 based on visual analysis [S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "For ethnicity estimation, FaRL [46] (a CLIP-based model) was used for classification into the same groups as in CREMA-D."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "For age estimation, GPT-4 was used with the following prompt: \"Please provide the estimated age of the individual in this photo as a single integer between 0 and 130. It should not be a range. I understand that your response will be based on visual analysis and may not accurately reflect the actual age of the individual\""}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "E3 Gen fits its feature planes and trains the denoiser in a single stage to avoid the noisy patterns and artifacts introduced by two-stage training schemes, which can distract denoising networks and degrade generated output quality. This single-stage approach leverages diffusion priors to constrain latent codes and enables learning of unseen regions in the training data. The integration of fitting and denoising processes ensures better optimization of the UV features plane and improves generation quality. The method also achieves high-resolution real-time rendering, which is not possible with implicit representations used in two-stage methods. This design choice supports expressive and editable avatar generation with superior efficiency and fidelity [S2][S4][S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "Different these methods, our UV features plane 𝑥𝑖 is constrained by both terms in the loss function. The introducing of denoising process constrain for UV feature plane optimization is beneficial for learning unseen regions in the training data as demonstrated by previous work [6]"},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "Another approach [40, 51] focuses on directly generating 3D representations, enabling faster inference. However, many of these methods adopt a two-stage training scheme. This can introduce noisy patterns and artifacts in the latent code due to the uncertain nature of inverse rendering. Consequently, these noisy patterns can distract denoising networks and affect the quality of the generated outputs."},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "We develop a part-aware deformation module that enables expressive full-body pose control."}
 ],
 "caveats": []}

