{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The public genuine/impostor avatar benchmark was built using three state-of-the-art avatar generators: GAGAvatar, LivePortrait, and HunyuanPortrait. The source video datasets used are CREMA-D and RAVDESS [S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "AVAPrintDB is constructed from two audiovisual corpora and three state-of-the-art avatar generators (GAGAvatar, LivePortrait, HunyuanPortrait), representing different synthesis paradigms"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "We select two widely used video datasets in the literature as they consider realistic scenarios for avatar fingerprinting: CREMA-D [20] and RAVDESS [21]"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The lightweight GCN verifier represents facial motion as a graph structure encoding mesh-like geometry, enabling spatial awareness and parameter efficiency by preserving local neighborhood relationships among facial regions [S2]. In contrast, NVIDIA's approach relies on high-dimensional pairwise distance tensors derived from all-pair landmark distances, which suffer from quadratic growth in input size and lack explicit spatial modeling [S2]. The GCN approach avoids this by aggregating information through message-passing layers, naturally modeling coordinated facial movements such as simultaneous eyebrow raises or lip articulations [S3]. This results in better spatial awareness and faster training and inference compared to the pairwise distance method [S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "our approach is well suited for facial motion modeling as it explicitly encodes the mesh-like geometry of the face through graph structure"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "In contrast to approaches that rely on high-dimensional pairwise distance tensors (e.g., all-pairs landmark distances as in [29])"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "GNNs are particularly well suited for facial motion modeling because they explicitly encode the mesh-like geometry of the face through a graph structure"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "a graph representation avoids quadratic growth in input size and facilitates faster training and inference"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "this convolution-like propagation naturally models coordinated facial movements, such as simultaneous eyebrow raises or lip articulations"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "InstructAvatar is trained on a combination of the MEAD, CC v1, and HDTF datasets [S1]. The MEAD dataset provides emotional talking face data with 8 emotion types and 3 intensity levels [S1]. The CC v1 dataset offers paired instruction-action video data for facial motion control [S1]. The HDTF dataset contributes high-quality talking face recordings to ensure effective lip synchronization [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "we leveraged the CC v1 dataset [13], which offers paired data comprising instructions and corresponding action videos. To ensure effective lip synchronization, we also incorporated the HDTF dataset [53], which has high-quality talking face recordings. Our model was trained on a combination of these three datasets"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "For emotional talking control, we augment the MEAD dataset [39] following the methods outlined in Sec. 3.2"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Our model was trained on a combination of these three datasets, enabling it to process either audio inputs or textual instructions"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "InstructAvatar's motion generator uses Conformer [11] as its backbone and CLIP-L/14 as its text encoder [S2][S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we apply CLIP-L/14 [29], and the Adapters are two layers MLPs"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "The motion generator, based on diffusion models [16], consists of 12 Conformer [11] blocks with a hidden state size of 768"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "MotionDiffuse replaces standard self-attention with cross-attention to inject text features into the motion sequence generation process, enabling text-conditioned motion synthesis [S1]. This modification allows the model to explicitly fuse text information with motion features during denoising steps, improving control and diversity in motion generation [S1]. The cross-attention mechanism replaces the key and value matrices in self-attention with those derived from text features, ensuring the motion sequence is guided by textual descriptions [S1]. This design supports more flexible and semantically meaningful motion generation compared to standard self-attention [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Cross-attention replaces X in K and V calculation by the text feature."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Real3D-Portrait uses the CelebV-HQ dataset to train its motion adapter and HTB-SR model, which is about 65 hours long and contains 35,666 video clips with 15,653 identities [S1]. For the audio-to-motion (A2M) model, it uses the VoxCeleb2 dataset, a low-fidelity but 2,000-hour-long lip-reading dataset to ensure generalizability [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "we use a high-fidelity talking face video dataset, CelebV-HQ (Zhu et al., 2022), which is about 65 hours and contains 35,666 video clips with a resolution of 512×512 involving 15,653 identities"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "we use VoxCeleb2 (Chung et al., 2018), a low-fidelity but 2,000-hour-long large-scale lip-reading dataset to guarantee the generalizability of the audio-tomotion mapping"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The image-to-plane (I2P) network includes both a ViT branch and a VGG branch to jointly handle 3D reconstruction and appearance fidelity: the ViT branch performs efficient pixel-to-world coordinate transformation and captures global structural features via attention, while the VGG branch extracts high-frequency texture details through convolutional layers to preserve fine appearance details in the rendered face [S1]. The two branches are fused to combine global structure and local texture, ensuring both accurate 3D reconstruction and visual fidelity [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "the ViT branch executes attention among patches and could efficiently handle the pixel-to-world canonicalization process. Since ViT cannot maintain high-frequency texture due to the patch embedding operation, as a complementary, we design a VGG branch, which is simply a stack of convolution layers to extract high-frequency appearance features."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "we design a hybrid network consisting of a ViT and a VGG branches. The ViT branch comprises a stack of SegFormer blocks, which executes attention among patches and could efficiently handle the pixel-to-world canonicalization process. Since ViT cannot maintain high-frequency texture due to the patch embedding operation, as a complementary, we design a VGG branch, which is simply a stack of convolution layers to extract high-frequency appearance features."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Text2Avatar segments the rendered human body to convert local attribute information into global context, which enhances the CLIP model's ability to match attributes accurately by reducing confusion from local visual details [S4]. The segmentation module improves attribute matching by providing a more coherent and globally consistent representation of the human body [S7]. This process supports the CLIP model in identifying the most relevant textual descriptions for each body segment, thereby improving the accuracy of attribute encoding [S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "we employed a segmentation module to support the CLIP model"},
    {"source_id": "S7", "quote": "the segmentation operation and codebook significantly improve the recognition accuracy and R-precision"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "we utilize CLIP to extract the decoupled attribute feature... by utilizing pre-trained image encoder... to identify the most relevant textual description"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}
